Every clinician should reserve an ER-LA-opioid for a severe-continuous-pain.
If a patient does not provably receive a certain-daily-immediate-release-opioid-dosage during at least 1 week and there is a clinician then it is recommended that the clinician does not consider an ER-LA-opioid for the patient.
Every immediate-release-oral-morphine-dosage that includes at least 60 morphine-milligrams-per-day is a certain-daily-immediate-release-opioid-dosage.
Every immediate-release-oral-oxycodone-dosage that includes at least 30 oxycodone-milligrams-per-day is a certain-daily-immediate-release-opioid-dosage.
Every equianalgesic-immediate-release-opioid-dosage is a certain-daily-immediate-release-opioid-dosage.
If a patient has a pain and the pain is not a severe-continuous-pain and there is a clinician then it is recommended that the clinician does not prescribe an ER-LA-opioid for the pain.
